//story: Parent app — Native iOS and Android apps separated family money from personal accounts. Blue marked shared finances and neutral marked personal ones, so owners could control family spending while each member kept their own.
//story: Child app — The child app stayed savings-first: a goal-based piggy bank, tasks, and history. It taught money in small steps, and a parent could not open a child’s savings without permission.
//story: Full bank — Single users still needed a complete bank, so ordering a card, payments, deposits, loans, and loyalty lived in the same product as the family roles.


[two-apps]
## Two Apps, One Banking System

//problem: The team had to deliver native parent and child apps, including the complete banking core, within a reduced MVP timeline.
//research: We prioritized essential scenarios and identified reusable patterns across accounts, cards, transactions, payments, and family features.
//iterations: We built a shared design foundation while adapting navigation, content, and functionality to each audience.
//outcome: Two connected native apps with a consistent product language, released in six months instead of the initially planned eight.

//gallery: two-apps-child.png, two-apps-family.png, two-apps-savings.png


[family-finances]
## Family and Personal Finances

//problem: Parents needed to manage shared family money without mixing it with their personal accounts, while different family members required different levels of access.
//research: We mapped family roles, permissions, spending scenarios, and the needs of both family and single users.
//iterations: We separated family and personal finances through account structure, permissions, and color-coded interfaces.
//outcome: A clear family banking model where owners could control shared spending while each member retained access to their own finances.

//gallery: child-cards.png, family-account.png, personal-account.png


[banking-children]
## Banking for Children

//problem: The child app needed to teach financial responsibility without becoming too complex, restrictive, or dependent on constant parental control.
//research: We explored via survey how parents introduced money to children and found different needs across age groups.
//iterations: We designed rewarded tasks, goal-based piggy banks, child-friendly copy, and age-appropriate controls with parental approval.
//outcome: A savings-first experience that gave children more independence while keeping parents informed and in control.

//gallery: child-theme.png, child-piggy.png, child-task.png


[payment-scenarios]
## Complex Payment Scenarios

//problem: The bank supported a wide range of service providers, many of which returned inconsistent or unstructured payment data.
//research: We analyzed key payment types, repeat-payment behavior, provider requirements, and common input patterns.
//iterations: We introduced adaptive local forms, autocomplete, recent-activity templates, and a consistent structure across payment and transfer flows.
//outcome: A scalable payment experience that supported diverse providers while remaining predictable and easy to use.

